<Article
  title="Constantinople and the Adventurous Mat: The New Scythe Faction"
  description="The first new faction in years, it uses stars as a resource — and it works in regular Scythe."
  game="duel-of-meloch"
  gameTitle="Duel of Meloch"
  category="Strategy"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Scythe has had five base factions and two from Invaders from Afar for a long
    time, and the faction roster felt settled. Duel of Meloch changes that: inside
    the box is <strong>Constantinople</strong>, the first brand-new Scythe faction
    in years, plus <strong>Adventurous</strong>, the eighth and final player mat.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Constantinople plays in light gray and uniquely uses stars as a resource. It ships with the Adventurous mat — the last remaining top/bottom action configuration — and both drop straight into normal 1–7 player Scythe." />

  <h2>Stars as a Resource</h2>

  <p>
    In every other version of Scythe, stars are pure output. You place them to
    mark achievements, six of them ends the game, and they convert into coins at
    scoring. They are the scoreboard, and nothing else.
  </p>

  <p>
    Constantinople treats them as something you can <strong>spend</strong>. That is
    a genuine departure — it takes the game's victory counter and makes it part of
    your economy.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Why that's a bigger deal than it sounds">
    Scythe's whole tension is that placing stars advances you toward triggering the
    end while your coin position may not be ready. A faction that can spend stars
    is playing with the game's clock as a currency. That interacts with every
    other faction at the table, not just its own board.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Adventurous Mat</h2>

  <p>
    Player mats in Scythe pair a top action with a bottom action in each column,
    and the seven existing mats cover seven of the possible arrangements.
    Adventurous is the eighth — the last configuration of top and bottom actions
    not found on any of the others.
  </p>

  <p>
    That completeness is the appeal. Long-time players have known there was one
    combination left unbuilt, and this is it. In a game where the faction/mat
    pairing matters more than the faction alone, adding an eighth mat widens the
    combination space for <em>every</em> faction you own, not just the new one.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="The mat may matter more than the faction">
    This is the standing lesson of Scythe and it applies here too. A new mat that
    can pair with any of your existing factions arguably has more effect on your
    collection than a new faction that can only be one player. Don't overlook it
    just because Constantinople is the headline.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Using Them in Regular Scythe</h2>

  <p>
    Both work in your normal games. Constantinople and Adventurous slot into
    standard 1–7 player Scythe exactly as any other faction and mat would, and
    everything travels the other way too — all your existing factions, mats,
    encounter cards and factory cards work inside Duel of Meloch.
  </p>

  <p>
    For Scythe owners, this reframes the purchase entirely. You're not only buying
    a two-player duel; you're buying a faction expansion that happens to come with
    a duel game attached. Depending on how much two-player gaming you do, either
    half can be the reason you bought it.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Light gray, not white">
    Constantinople's components are light gray, deliberately distinct from
    Polania's white. Worth knowing before a seven-player game — and worth a
    sentence when you're teaching, because at a glance across a busy table they're
    the two that get confused.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>How Strong Is It?</h2>

  <p>
    Nobody credibly knows yet, and that's the honest answer. Duel of Meloch has
    only been out since July 2026. Faction strength in Scythe is judged over
    hundreds of games by a community that argues about it seriously, and
    Constantinople hasn't had that yet.
  </p>

  <p>
    What can be said is structural: a faction that spends stars is playing a
    different resource game to everyone else at the table, and that kind of
    mechanical outlier tends to be either underrated for months or overrated
    immediately. Play it a dozen times before forming an opinion, and treat any
    confident tier placement you read right now with suspicion — including ours.
  </p>

  <p>
    Our take on how the established factions rank is in the
    <a href="/games/scythe/faction-tier-list/">Scythe faction tier list</a>, which
    covers Constantinople with the same caveat.
  </p>

  <h2>Is the Faction Alone Worth the Box?</h2>

  <p>
    If you play Scythe often and multiplayer, a new faction plus the last player
    mat is a meaningful addition — you're getting an expansion's worth of content
    with a full second game alongside it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If Scythe comes out twice a year, probably not on its own. The case then rests
    on whether you want the duel, which is covered in
    <a href="/games/duel-of-meloch/is-duel-of-meloch-worth-it/">is Duel of Meloch worth it</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
